  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Below normal low temperatures give way to a \nwarming trend early this week. \n\nHigh pressure will drift southwest through Monday. As a result,\nexpect a continuation of frost and freeze conditions during the\novernight periods with seasonable temperatures during the \ndaylight hours.\n\nAfter a chilly start, temperatures will rebound well into the\n60s for much of the area this afternoon. Some stratocumulus are\npossible at times, but may ultimately be thin and transient.\n\nHigh pressure will gradually drift southwest toward the central \nAppalachians tonight into Monday morning. Low-level flow will \nturn westerly allowing for less of a marine influence. This \nshould set us up for one more good radiational cooling night \nbefore temperatures warm under return flow early in the \nworkweek. Tonight will be more of a ridge-valley split type of \nnight with cooler conditions in the valleys/cold pockets/locations\nalong and east of I-95 compared to the ridge tops given the \nsharp surface inversion/decoupling.\n\nOnly minor changes have been made to the previous min temperature \nforecasts, which are near or below the coldest guidance which \nperforms best in radiational cooling (MOS, NBM 10th percentile and \nCanadian Regional).\n\nExpect temperatures to rise above normal (upper 60s to lower 70s) by \nMonday afternoon as the warming airmass mixes to the surface. \nMinimal cloud cover and light winds will remain persistent.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Above normal temperatures and dry conditions \ncontinue through the end of the week.\n\nAn upper-level ridge will build through the middle of the week \nsupporting above normal temperatures. A dry cold front will \ncross the area Tuesday, only dropping temperatures slightly. \nHigh pressure settles south of the area during the second half \nof the week supporting a warming trend again. A weak upper-level\ndisturbance will traverse the region Thursday night only \nbringing high clouds ahead of it. It looks like no precipitation \nwill occur through the remainder of October.",
